Square brackets construct text, a homogeneous sequence or a map. A binding,
assignment, parameter, return, field, field default or collection entry can
supply the immediate receiving type.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    counts = [1, 2]
    title = ["Build", " log"]
    labels [String] = ["Build", "log"]
    text String = [42]
    bytes [Byte] = [77, 79]
    scores [String = I32] = ["Priya" = 10]
]

Without a receiver, an all-String head concatenates. Homogeneous non-String
values form a sequence. A known sequence and compatible scalar contributions
form a sequence of its element type. A direct key/value entry establishes a
map. An incompatible mix such as `["[1, \"hello\"]"]` needs an explicit compatible
receiver. String formatting applies only once the String context is known.

A declaration fixes the construction kind and nesting shape. Passing the value
later cannot change either. Raw numeric leaves inside a known shape may still
receive an exact numeric type under @../numbers/#numeric-literals (Numeric
literals). Whole and decimal literal origins select the binary-float family
together in either order. Already typed items keep their types.

### Splicing and nesting

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    first = [1, 2]
    second = [3, 4]
    flat = [first, second, 5]
    rows = [[first], [second]]
    one_row = [[first, second]]
    tags = [["Home", "Docs"], ["API"]]
]

`flat` contains five integers. A sequence contributes its elements in order
and a scalar contributes one element. A direct nested plain ordered head preserves a
dimension. `rows` contains two fresh rows, `one_row` contains one row of four
integers and `tags` contains rows of separate Strings.

A plain ordered head has no body, keyed entry or independent structural
contract. Its directly nested use creates a fresh row even when every leaf
is String. Grouping parentheses do not change that role. A String receiver
cannot turn such a row into text, and incompatible rows and scalars cannot
share a collection.

A child with independently established String or map output contributes its
completed value instead. String formatters, non-whitespace raw body text and
String-producing wrappers establish String evidence. A keyed child constructs
a map.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    words [String] = [[:Hello], [:World]]
    message = [[:Hello], [:World]]
    scores = [["Priya" = 10], ["Rob" = 8]]
]

`words` contains two Strings. `message` joins them into `"HelloWorld"`.
`scores` contains two maps, not two rows of map entries. A bracket inside a
call argument, directive operand or routed slot argument follows that
operand's receiving contract instead of establishing an outer dimension.

Splicing reads membership exactly once at the written position. New membership
is independent, while reference-valued elements keep their usual sharing.
To store an existing row itself, insert it with `~matrix.push(row)`. Splicing a
matrix contributes its rows, keeping their identities. Later effects cannot
change which items an earlier splice read. On failure, the receiving binding
gets no partial result. Earlier explicit effects still stand.

### Empty values and bodies

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    title String = []
    labels [String] = []
    blank [I32] = [:   ]
    rows [[I32]] = [[]]
    other_rows = [[], [1, 2]]
    scores [String = I32] = [=]
]

An empty construction or a body containing only layout whitespace needs type
evidence. Neither a colon nor formatting whitespace establishes String. An
empty construction with a String receiver produces the empty String. A String
body containing whitespace follows its ordinary formatting rules. A typed
sequence body with no children produces zero items, subject to the ordinary
empty-value rules for its collection type.
The first row of `other_rows` takes its element type from the second row.
An empty map requires `["[=]"]` and evidence for both types. A map
receiver rejects `["[]"]`. An optional receiver can wrap a completed
empty value as present. Only `none` denotes absence.

A sequence body accepts child constructions and structural control flow.
Formatting whitespace contributes nothing. Raw text belongs in a String child.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    greet |names [String]| -> [String] {
        return [loop names |name|:
            [: Hello, [name]!]
        ]
    }
]

Each iteration contributes one greeting. See @../templates (Templates) for
typed slots and wrappers and @#hash-maps (Hash maps) for keyed construction.
MON uses the same delimiters for literal containers and preserves every
authored sequence element. See @../mon/#mon-format (MON format).
